<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vasilko Konstantinovich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1209–1238</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vasilko Konstantinovich was prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> from 1218 to 1238, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-of-rostov">Konstantin of Rostov</a>. He fought with the forces of Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-sit-river">Battle of the Sit River</a> in March 1238 and was captured. According to the chronicles he refused to serve the Mongols and was killed, and he was later venerated as a saint. His descendants continued to rule Rostov.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasilko-konstantinovich.jpg" alt="Vasilko Konstantinovich" width="320" height="591" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Russian manuscript illuminators, 1560-1570s Facial Chronicle (Illustrated Chronicle of Ivan the Terrible) (... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%92%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%BE_%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87_%D0%B8%D0%B4%D1%91%D1%82_%D0%BA_%D0%9A%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%BA%D0%B5,_1223_%D0%B3.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
